A||||5||Oreif||Fuel Regulator Questions.||||5||
Z||000000||Oreif||07-08-2000||10:21 AM||||I have a 2.8L in an 86 SE. I have a big bore throttle body (ported intake), Sprint manifolds and this winter will be upgrading the exhaust system. My question is do I need an adjustable fuel pressure regulator? Where do I buy one? Who makes them? I have heard that if you upgrade intake and exhaust the adjustable regulator is basically a must.<BR>TIA<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Vader||07-08-2000||11:03 AM||||Oreif,<P>I presume your MPFI has stock injectors.  That being the case, the fuel pressure should be at least checked to determine the factory setting.  Since there is a tolerable range for factory pressure, you may be on the low end.  Any modification may require fuel delivery rates beyond the factory intention, and would indicate the need for higher pressure or larger injectors.  Typically, the stock injectors will suffice until the cam profile is changed significantly or a power adder is used, such as a supercharger or nitrous system.  If you suspect a lean condition at heavy loads, you can check O2 sensor crosscounts with a Diacom or AutoXray scanner to determine fuel rates at WOT.<P>If your sensor indicates a lean condition, it is relatively easy to install an AFPR.  Better yet, it is quite easy to convert the factory FPR to an adjustable unit.<P>Check this guide:<BR><A HREF="http://homestead.juno.com/jlstrey/files/AFPR.pdf" TARGET=_blank>AFPR.pdf</A><P>You'll need Adobe Acrobat Reader to view/print the file.<P>Later,<BR>Vader<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Make Me Bad</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||07-08-2000||11:54 AM||||Thanks, I read thru it and it answered most of the questions I had.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||HYPTNOTiSE||07-08-2000||02:50 PM||||if you're going to go with an adjustable fuel pressure regulator, get one for mid 80's MPFI systems. They're sold for camaro and firebird engines, but it's the same AFPR. Holley sells a nice one, that's what i have.  instead of a sucky allen screw that's like impossible to adjust cos the AFPR is under the plenum it has a dial that you can turn with your finger. Pretty slick and about 100 bucks. Also get a new fuel pump. Get a universal 255lph fuel pump from jegs, i think they're 100 bucks as well. Fiero.f-body, grand national, and talons, use the same fuel pump.<P>------------------<BR>Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ nitrous||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierosound||07-08-2000||04:50 PM||||HYPNOTise...<P>Could you post the Holley Part Number for that AFPR??  I've been wanting to get it, but can't find the PN for it. Thanks...<P>||||reg||5
Z||000005||HYPTNOTiSE||07-09-2000||11:58 AM||||It's in the Lt-1/Ls-1 section of Jeg's high performance. for 85-92 TPI the part number is 510-512-501.  I ordered my AFPR from Joe Wynman, because i thought he had custom made his own for some reason. I was looking through jegs and i saw in the catalog the exact setup he sent me.  Everything is really straight forward, but remember when you tighten the screws up make sure each screw is 8-10 inch pounds.<P>------------------<BR>Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ nitrous||||reg||1
